<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> I assume that a ROMable Forth...
<[Gary] GARY-S> UUCP user network - it is tied into Internet (TCP/IP) (arpanet) 
that you have at LA
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> runs on a PC or whatever, and then metacompiles into...
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> a small image that can be burned into ROM.
<[Gary] GARY-S> You are the user here ken..
<[Gary] GARY-S> ga
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> MAX-FORTH is a full interpreter running in 4k of ROM.  The 
way I use it is to compile into ram on top of the existing kernal.  I
soter variables in a separate region of memory that will remain ram in the 
final system.
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> The whole package is compiled and I copy out the program 
section into a ROM at the same address that the Ram occupied.
<[Gary] GARY-S> len ?
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> I see. Then it REALLY is a ROM based Forth...
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> and not merely a Romable one.
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> Yes,  in 4K complete with disk capability but no assembler,
  That takes another 4K and has to exist outside of the rom built into
 the chip.  The smallest package I have built has one chip, the processor, a 
crystal a resitor 2 caps and a detector.  I takes 20 ma or 6 ma if the pr
ocessor is in a wait for interrupt state.
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Thanks
<[Gary] GARY-S> ken - let me suggest you break up your longer...
<[Gary] GARY-S> messages into segments with the three dots...
<[Gary] GARY-S> indicating more to come..
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> Sounds good to me...
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> You have answered my question...
